# Build-context exclusions. Two goals: keep the context small (the host data
# directory alone is multi-GB) and make the image reproducible by never
# shipping host build output, local state, or secrets.

# Version control and host build output — everything is rebuilt in the image.
.git
.gitignore
.gitmodules
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# Runtime state and credentials. The data directory is bind-mounted at run time
# instead; `.sciencediscovery-data/` is the repository default and `data/` is the
# former one, still excluded so an un-migrated checkout cannot ship its tokens
# and `model-secrets.key` in the image.
# `.deer-flow/` is state the retired vendor harness used to write beside the
# services; the pattern stays so a stale directory on a developer machine
# cannot re-enter the build context. Unlike .gitignore, Docker patterns are
# anchored at the context root, so it needs `**`.
.sciencediscovery-data/
data/
**/.deer-flow/
**/.env
# This also excludes build-time files such as apps/web/.env.production. If the
# build ever requires one, pass its non-secret values explicitly instead.
**/.env.*
**/config.yaml
**/conf.yaml
.tmp/
.e2e/
.local/
.uv-cache/
.worktrees/
**/*.log

# Local tooling / editor / agent configuration, never part of the runtime.
# `.npmrc` is deliberately kept: it carries the workspace's pnpm settings.
.idea/
.vscode/
.claude/
.codex/
.agents/
.mcp.json
.ripgreprc

# Docs and integration tests are not needed by the running services.
docs/
.github/

# One exception under test/: the tagged-test adapter. Every package tsconfig
# includes src/**/*.ts, so the image's `pnpm build` type-checks the ~299
# *.test.ts files that import test/support/tagged/compat.mjs, and a module
# outside the context is `error TS2307` — which is how the Docker image job
# failed. `compat.d.mts` refers to nothing but `node:test`, so this directory
# closes over itself and admitting it pulls nothing else in.
#
# Docker never descends into an excluded directory, so `test/` followed by
# `!test/support/tagged` would not bring it back. The contents are excluded one
# level at a time and each parent is re-admitted on the way down, which is the
# only form Docker honours.
test/*
!test/support
test/support/*
!test/support/tagged

# Everything else under test/ stays out: the Playwright journeys, their
# helpers and fixtures are never needed by a running service, and the runtime
# stage is `COPY --from=builder /app /app`, so anything admitted here ships.
# The two patterns below re-state the language-runtime exclusions inside the
# directory just re-admitted — the last matching pattern wins, so a developer
# machine's __pycache__ or .venv there would otherwise ride along.
test/support/tagged/**/__pycache__
test/support/tagged/**/.venv

# Docker inputs themselves.
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore
